every time i told anyone that i was  working on dogs  the first thing they would do was pull  out their smartphone show me a picture  of their dog and tell me all about it  and i'm sitting there as a scientist  going that's that's all the information  that i want is everything you just told  me but but it's not written down  anywhere darwin's arc is our community  science project where we reach out  directly to dog owners and ask them to  participate in our research  what we're increasingly seeing is  science like this project which is a  citizen science project where owners are  more than happy to spend two hours in  front of their computer and answer 117  questions about their dog more than  happy to get that q-tip and do the swab  i reached out to the community of  applied animal behavior experts and  these are people who work in things like  dog training  and i said we're interested in traits  that differ between breeds were easy for  owners to identify  and that people were unlikely to try and  train their dog to do it or train their  dog not to do it basically we want  things that are genetic that are  heritable and they came up with this  fantastic list of questions  does your dog like to play with toys and  is your dog friendly with strangers but  there were also questions like  does your dog circle before he poops  when you have such a huge data set like  this they're looking at descriptions of  thousands of dogs and genome sequences  of almost 2 000 dogs  stuff is going to start to shake out we  found out that behavior in dogs is very  complicated which is not surprising  behavior and everything is very  complicated so we have these behavioral  factors as we scored each dog on and  each factor is actually a bunch of  different survey questions that are all  asking sort of the same thing what we  saw out of these eight factors is that  there were a few of them like whether  your dog was interested in playing with  toys and how trainable your dog was some  of the breeds seemed to be more  different from other dogs on those  particular traits and then there were  others that seemed to have much less of  a signal off of them and one of the ones  that didn't really seem to differ  between breeds much at all the one that  actually scored the lowest out of those  eight factors was one that we'd called  agonistic threshold and that's the kind  of set of questions it's all related to  to how your dog reacts to something it's  surprising or unexpected in their  environment including whether they kind  of react aggressively to it or not there  doesn't really seem to be much there it  seems like that's got a pretty large  environmental component to it  you can't really  predict what an individual dog's  behavior is going to be like accurately  from their breed ancestry alone it's  much too complicated  most of the breeds we think of today  whether it's german shepherds or  labrador retrievers or border collies  traced back only about 160 years to the  victorian era where there was a lot of  really intensive greeting for what dogs  looked like and dog shows were getting  really popular that's a very short  period of time in terms of evolution  but it turns out that dogs were  domesticated from wolves  something like 10 or 20 000 years ago  and so probably at some point people  started  favoring dogs that had behaviors that  were useful to them  eventually you had these kind of working  populations of dogs you have herding  dogs you have livestock guarding dogs  you've got guard dogs  and those have probably been around for  a very long time if you start with a  group of dogs that are bred as a working  population of herding dogs and you  establish a breed from them you will  have a population of dogs that are more  likely to be hurting  type behaviors  one of the reasons that we study dog  genetics at all is that we're interested  in whether understanding how genes shape  behaviors and behavioral disorders in  dogs might actually help us learn  something about things like psychiatric  diseases in people  dogs look a lot different than us  but they actually share the vast  majority of their genes with us and so  it's always really surprising to sort of  break down these species barriers one of  the really cool things that the  researchers also found was they found at  least 11 new spots in dog dna that are  tied to behavior and there's found some  really cool links to human behavior so  they found a new region of dog dna that  seems to be involved with howling and  it's related to respond human dna that's  involved in speech and language they  found a new spot in dog dna that seems  to be related to human social ability  which seems to be related to learning in  humans  two thousand dogs is a pretty remarkable  number to be able to do that with for  context in humans when they study  complex behaviors they often need study  sizes of hundreds of thousands and even  millions of people i encourage everybody  to go sign up for darwin's arc because  the more dogs the better at this point  in time somebody asked me once how many  dogs i wanted and i said well you know  ideally all of them would be great  while this study is certainly  surprising and bound to be controversial  even the authors admit you know this is  just the beginning and potentially with  larger sample sizes they may start to  see correlations that they're not seeing  now the more genetic information the  more of a foothold other scientists are  going to have for  teasing out these genes that are  responsible for behavior or potentially  psychological issues or behavioral  issues and things like that both in dogs  and in people  well it's not really a secret anymore  because i keep telling everybody but but  my dirty secret as a dog genetics person  was that i  have never actually owned a dog i own  three cats they are fantastic  and i would love to have a cat genetics  project one day
 

